    {
      "id": "GgwuBt_mfvuDqYNXDwXGI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3503.296880299871,
      "y": -104.20550374285159,
      "width": 1106.578857421875,
      "height": 2600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1273025005,
      "version": 5291,
      "versionNonce": 1289724020,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1764178235414,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Pre-vLLM serving systems fall short of managing the KV cache memory efficiently: they store the KV cache \nof a request in contiguous memory space --> allocation for worst case scenario strategy\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDue to the unpredictable output lengths from the LLM, they pre-allocate a contiguous chunk of memory with \nthe request’s maximum length irrespective of the actual input or eventual output length of the request. The \nrequest may end beforehand and yet other shorter requests cannot utilize any part of the chunk that is \ncurrently unused.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nPagedAttention partitions the KV cache of each sequence into KV blocks. Each block contains the key and value\nvectors for a fixed number of tokens. Hence, GPU memory slots are allocated on demand to reduce such internal \nfragmentation.  This allows storing continuous keys and values in non-contiguous memory space.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow we reuse the fetch the KVs from cache for each request and compute only one new token per sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is possible because in CPU is also maintained the block tables—the mapping between logical and physical KV \nblocks of each request. Each block table entry records the corresponding physical blocks of a logical block and \nthe number of filled positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach Transformer layer will have its KV blocks\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis design alleviates internal fragmentation by using relatively small blocks and allocating them on demand.\nAlson vLLM achieves continuos batching as it continuously schedules a new request when a request in \nthe processing batch completes and slots are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWith this setup (and other advanced features chunked prefill, prefix caching, guided & speculative decoding, \ndisaggregated P/D) vLLM improves the LLM serving throughput by 2-4× compared to the previous \nstate-of-the-art systems.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Pre-vLLM serving systems fall short of managing the KV cache memory efficiently: they store the KV cache \nof a request in contiguous memory space --> allocation for worst case scenario strategy\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDue to the unpredictable output lengths from the LLM, they pre-allocate a contiguous chunk of memory with \nthe request’s maximum length irrespective of the actual input or eventual output length of the request. The \nrequest may end beforehand and yet other shorter requests cannot utilize any part of the chunk that is \ncurrently unused.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nPagedAttention partitions the KV cache of each sequence into KV blocks. Each block contains the key and value\nvectors for a fixed number of tokens. Hence, GPU memory slots are allocated on demand to reduce such internal \nfragmentation.  This allows storing continuous keys and values in non-contiguous memory space.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow we reuse the fetch the KVs from cache for each request and compute only one new token per sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is possible because in CPU is also maintained the block tables—the mapping between logical and physical KV \nblocks of each request. Each block table entry records the corresponding physical blocks of a logical block and \nthe number of filled positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach Transformer layer will have its KV blocks\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis design alleviates internal fragmentation by using relatively small blocks and allocating them on demand.\nAlson vLLM achieves continuos batching as it continuously schedules a new request when a request in \nthe processing batch completes and slots are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWith this setup (and other advanced features chunked prefill, prefix caching, guided & speculative decoding, \ndisaggregated P/D) vLLM improves the LLM serving throughput by 2-4× compared to the previous \nstate-of-the-art systems.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "E46RyzBpw-c77K64PPjD_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 995.1296735636811,
      "y": 2522.3909755581553,
      "width": 805.6166538900814,
      "height": 49.428571428570685,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "p63USiADxTqnUsw_OibuP"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0C4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1564908058,
      "version": 4530,
      "versionNonce": 754552855,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763628416541,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Memory-bound vs Compute-bound",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Memory-bound vs Compute-bound",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pUCIRhPI-Jtcdt9iGigbb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 995.0631883825622,
      "y": 2569.1762489738567,
      "width": 1044.5989990234375,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0C8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1709455450,
      "version": 3107,
      "versionNonce": 1906144567,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763628416541,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "As a result, this phase severely underutilizes GPU --> GPUs excel at doing many large, independent matrix \noperations at once. But in this sequential generation phase the model multiplies a large matrix (the model \nweights) by a single vector (the new token’s hidden state). This is matrix–vector multiplication, not \nmatrix–matrix.\n\nThe main bottleneck is not computation but data logistics. computation and becomes memory-bound, being \nresponsible for most portion of the latency of a single request.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn serving LLMs with batching instead of doing one request at a time you can combine them into one big \nbatch and perform a matrix operation again.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "As a result, this phase severely underutilizes GPU --> GPUs excel at doing many large, independent matrix \noperations at once. But in this sequential generation phase the model multiplies a large matrix (the model \nweights) by a single vector (the new token’s hidden state). This is matrix–vector multiplication, not \nmatrix–matrix.\n\nThe main bottleneck is not computation but data logistics. computation and becomes memory-bound, being \nresponsible for most portion of the latency of a single request.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn serving LLMs with batching instead of doing one request at a time you can combine them into one big \nbatch and perform a matrix operation again.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
