    {
      "id": "HE2weL8Uf-4d4540gxP-r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1438.9039273148533,
      "y": 2780.3729198239744,
      "width": 595.3594970703126,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 908543558,
      "version": 4211,
      "versionNonce": 1694842233,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763628666159,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "KV cache is in storage so at each decoding step:\n    1. move Wk, Wq, and Wv from storage to compute and \n        multiply with dense vector of last token x\n    2. move KV cache to compute and multiply Qi with K, \n        and then with V\n    3. move attention score back to storage\n\nIt turn out that moving data in the GPU is way \nslower then the actual operation of multiplying the \nx vector for the model weights. That is why the \ncomputational capacity of the GPU is not fully used.\n\nTo solve this multiple sequences are batched together and\ntheir KVcache stored on GPU. Hence, with Kv-cache decoding\nmove from being compute-bound to memory-bound problem. \nThe speed of deconding depends on how many data we can \ncache in the GPU memory.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "KV cache is in storage so at each decoding step:\n    1. move Wk, Wq, and Wv from storage to compute and \n        multiply with dense vector of last token x\n    2. move KV cache to compute and multiply Qi with K, \n        and then with V\n    3. move attention score back to storage\n\nIt turn out that moving data in the GPU is way \nslower then the actual operation of multiplying the \nx vector for the model weights. That is why the \ncomputational capacity of the GPU is not fully used.\n\nTo solve this multiple sequences are batched together and\ntheir KVcache stored on GPU. Hence, with Kv-cache decoding \nmove from being compute-bound to memory-bound problem. \nThe speed of deconding depends on how many data we can \ncache in the GPU memory.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "M-Hmxj6-Bd0qISnf8kp1V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1057.159194103176,
      "y": -118.87495554859441,
      "width": 805.6166538900814,
      "height": 49.428571428570685,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "ERjqRIM40xyyIFau0Ih96",
        "pyCTV9wf0pZ4D3JfC7wD4"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0gV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1177991066,
      "version": 4181,
      "versionNonce": 1399059072,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763641911811,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "LLM inference memory refresh",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "LLM inference memory refresh",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YbITK5ceQ3Ggmb1UK82-6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1056.1831180419808,
      "y": -62.87097790926049,
      "width": 1043.6990966796875,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "pyCTV9wf0pZ4D3JfC7wD4"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 564322054,
      "version": 3108,
      "versionNonce": 2062185856,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763641911811,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Each LLM inference request goes through two phases - a prefill phase followed by a decode phase. \nThe prefill phase corresponds to the processing of the input prompt and the decode phase corresponds \nto the autoregressive token generation.\n\nEssetioally this is what happens in the the prefill phase:\n    Step 1: Loading the model weights to GPU\n    Step 2: Tokenizing the prompt on CPU and transferring the token tensor to GPU\n    Step 3: Generating the first token of the completion by running the tokenized prompt through the \n        network --> FFN & Att matrix multiplications\n\nThe generated token is appended to the sequence of input tokens and used it as a new input to \ngenerate the second token of the completion. Then, repeat this process until either a stop sequence \nhas been generated (e.g. a single end-of-sequence (EOS) token) or the configured maximum sequence \nlength has been reached. This multi-step phase is usually called the generation phase, the decoding phase, \nthe auto-regressive phase.\n\nDecoding is therefore a prefill phase that is repeated over and over with longer input at each steps.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Each LLM inference request goes through two phases - a prefill phase followed by a decode phase. \nThe prefill phase corresponds to the processing of the input prompt and the decode phase corresponds \nto the autoregressive token generation.\n\nEssetioally this is what happens in the the prefill phase:\n    Step 1: Loading the model weights to GPU\n    Step 2: Tokenizing the prompt on CPU and transferring the token tensor to GPU\n    Step 3: Generating the first token of the completion by running the tokenized prompt through the \n        network --> FFN & Att matrix multiplications\n\nThe generated token is appended to the sequence of input tokens and used it as a new input to \ngenerate the second token of the completion. Then, repeat this process until either a stop sequence \nhas been generated (e.g. a single end-of-sequence (EOS) token) or the configured maximum sequence \nlength has been reached. This multi-step phase is usually called the generation phase, the decoding phase, \nthe auto-regressive phase.\n\nDecoding is therefore a prefill phase that is repeated over and over with longer input at each steps.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
