      "seed": 271547843,
      "version": 4718,
      "versionNonce": 414661943,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763552813955,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Batching and other serving problems",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Batching and other serving problems",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "MTzdYxCaNtehXjI6J7MqB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2138.340480488104,
      "y": 998.5298060909132,
      "width": 1135.2989501953125,
      "height": 2375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "XE0o94WgP62BtZMtEXgLQ"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 991278371,
      "version": 4555,
      "versionNonce": 1686050249,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763734578930,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Batching the requests to an LLM service is non-trivial....\n\nEarly-finished and late-joining requests. The engine maintains a batch of requests fixed until all requests \nin the batch finish. Since each request in a batch may require different number of iterations,\nresulting in certain requests finishing earlier than the others. Early return of the finished request to the \nclient, imposing a substantial amount of extra latency. This is because the engine only returns the execution \nresults to the serving system when it finishes processing all requests in the batch. \nWhen a new request arrives in the middle of the current batch’s execution, it has to wait until all requests \nin the current batch have finished.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInteraction-level scheduling works at the token level rather than at the request level. But it bring some \nchallenges in batching.\n\nThe requests may have vastly different input and output lengths. Recall that in order to batch the execution \nof multiple requests, the execution of each request must consist  of identical operations, each consuming \nidentically-shaped input tensors. There are three cases for a pair of requests where the next iteration cannot \nbe batched together:\n    (1) both requests are in the prefill phase and each has different number of input tokens\n    (2) both are in the decoding phase and each is processing a token at different index from each other\n    (3) each request is in the different phase: prefill or decoding. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA straightforward batching technique would pad the inputs and outputs of the requests to equalize their lengths, \nwasting GPU computation and memory. The solution proposed in the ORCA architecture is selective batching, which \napplies batching only to a selected set of operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNB: in the KV cache dev notebook this is not implemented :) we assume all sequences to have same length",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Batching the requests to an LLM service is non-trivial....\n\nEarly-finished and late-joining requests. The engine maintains a batch of requests fixed until all requests \nin the batch finish. Since each request in a batch may require different number of iterations,\nresulting in certain requests finishing earlier than the others. Early return of the finished request to the \nclient, imposing a substantial amount of extra latency. This is because the engine only returns the execution \nresults to the serving system when it finishes processing all requests in the batch. \nWhen a new request arrives in the middle of the current batch’s execution, it has to wait until all requests \nin the current batch have finished.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInteraction-level scheduling works at the token level rather than at the request level. But it bring some \nchallenges in batching.\n\nThe requests may have vastly different input and output lengths. Recall that in order to batch the execution \nof multiple requests, the execution of each request must consist  of identical operations, each consuming \nidentically-shaped input tensors. There are three cases for a pair of requests where the next iteration cannot \nbe batched together:\n    (1) both requests are in the prefill phase and each has different number of input tokens\n    (2) both are in the decoding phase and each is processing a token at different index from each other\n    (3) each request is in the different phase: prefill or decoding. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA straightforward batching technique would pad the inputs and outputs of the requests to equalize their lengths, \nwasting GPU computation and memory. The solution proposed in the ORCA architecture is selective batching, which \napplies batching only to a selected set of operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNB: in the KV cache dev notebook this is not implemented :) we assume all sequences to have same length",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
