    },
    {
      "id": "ChfPlvnk8ZpCJv0GvJMGH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1057.6827706774643,
      "y": 907.6314195295784,
      "width": 1007.9989013671875,
      "height": 1225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1811705094,
      "version": 3896,
      "versionNonce": 1720138489,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1763629116443,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This setup described involves expensive computations (attention mechanism scales quadratically in the \ntotal sequence length of the sequence), a lot of which being actually redundant. \n    ==> KV-cache\n\nIn a Transformer’s self-attention layer, each token produces three vectors:\n    Query (Q) — represents what this token is looking for\n    Key (K) — represents what this token contains\n    Value (V) — represents the actual content to be used if the key is relevant\n\nThe attention output for token i is computed as:\n\n\n\n\nThat is, each query qi interacts with all previous keys and values (K,V).\nWhen processing the prefill prompt (“Once upon a time”), we compute Q, K, V for all input tokens.\nTo generate token 6 (“there”), we:\n    1. Compute its own q6 k6 v6.\n    2. Compute attention using:\n\n                                q6 x [k1,k2,k3,k4,k5,k6]\nand then:\n                                    [v1,v2,v3,v4,v5,v6]\n\nThis requires all previous Keys and Values from tokens 1–5.\n\nEach new token depends on all the previous tokens, so:\n    - To predict token 7, you need the results from tokens 1–6.\n    - To predict token 8, you need results from tokens 1–7.\n    - etc.\n\nThis creates a data dependency — you can’t compute token 7 before you have token 6.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWith KV-cache attentoin mechanism scales linearly now!",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This setup described involves expensive computations (attention mechanism scales quadratically in the \ntotal sequence length of the sequence), a lot of which being actually redundant. \n    ==> KV-cache\n\nIn a Transformer’s self-attention layer, each token produces three vectors:\n    Query (Q) — represents what this token is looking for\n    Key (K) — represents what this token contains\n    Value (V) — represents the actual content to be used if the key is relevant\n\nThe attention output for token i is computed as:\n\n\n\n\nThat is, each query qi interacts with all previous keys and values (K,V).\nWhen processing the prefill prompt (“Once upon a time”), we compute Q, K, V for all input tokens.\nTo generate token 6 (“there”), we:\n    1. Compute its own q6 k6 v6.\n    2. Compute attention using:\n\n                                q6 x [k1,k2,k3,k4,k5,k6]\nand then:\n                                    [v1,v2,v3,v4,v5,v6]\n\nThis requires all previous Keys and Values from tokens 1–5.\n\nEach new token depends on all the previous tokens, so:\n    - To predict token 7, you need the results from tokens 1–6.\n    - To predict token 8, you need results from tokens 1–7.\n    - etc.\n\nThis creates a data dependency — you can’t compute token 7 before you have token 6.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWith KV-cache attentoin mechanism scales linearly now!",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
