      "text": "Basics of RESTful API",
      "fontSize": 39.54285714285655,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basics of RESTful API",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "140LOt5yaFY-IKiVU--i2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -96.08302025611647,
      "y": 732.2573593566703,
      "width": 1067.4791259765625,
      "height": 1125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "BMJDzbftjIR4WYqG8nNZI"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6g",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 15264354,
      "version": 3189,
      "versionNonce": 38171737,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "Y2JLUN3LvsNMmfcCH6bnZ",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1763628204675,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "RESTful APIs rely on HTTP methods—such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE—to perform actions \non resources, which are identified by URLs.\n\nThink of the Internet as a digital map in which each piece of content needs its unique address.\nA Uniform Resource Locator (URL) is like a name tag for anything you can find online. It's a string \nof characters that identifies a resource, whether that's a web page, a file, or even just an idea.\n\"https://bookstore.com/books/0451450523\".\n\nREST doesn’t invent a new communication method — instead, it uses HTTP’s existing structure and rules \nto manage how clients and servers exchange data. Each resource is given a unique URL, and the HTTP \nmethods define what kind of action to perform:\n    - GET — Retrieve data from the server (for example, GET /books to list all books).\n    - POST — Send new data to the server (e.g., POST /books to add a new book).\n    - PUT — Update an existing resource (e.g., PUT /books/3 to edit book #3).\n    - DELETE — Remove a resource (e.g., DELETE /books/3 to delete book #3).\n\ncURL (pronounced like \"curl\") is a free and open source CLI app for uploading and downloading individual files.\nPut simpler: in almost every internet-connected device (in 2020 also multiplanetary)\n\n                            \n\n\n\n\n\nIn an HTTP request (or response), the header is a collection of key–value pairs that provide metadata \nabout the communication. They do not contain the actual data you’re requesting — instead, they describe \nthe request itself.\n    - \"Accept: application/json\" header telling the server you expect a JSON response.\n    - \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_TOKEN\" header used for authentication (if the API is protected).\n\n\n\nIn HTTP, GET requests usually don’t have a body — they just fetch data. The body is the part after the \nheaders. It contains the actual content you’re sending to the server — usually structured data like JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere is an example from OpenAI guide for API chat completion endpoint:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "RESTful APIs rely on HTTP methods—such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE—to perform actions \non resources, which are identified by URLs.\n\nThink of the Internet as a digital map in which each piece of content needs its unique address.\nA Uniform Resource Locator (URL) is like a name tag for anything you can find online. It's a string \nof characters that identifies a resource, whether that's a web page, a file, or even just an idea.\n\"https://bookstore.com/books/0451450523\".\n\nREST doesn’t invent a new communication method — instead, it uses HTTP’s existing structure and rules \nto manage how clients and servers exchange data. Each resource is given a unique URL, and the HTTP \nmethods define what kind of action to perform:\n    - GET — Retrieve data from the server (for example, GET /books to list all books).\n    - POST — Send new data to the server (e.g., POST /books to add a new book).\n    - PUT — Update an existing resource (e.g., PUT /books/3 to edit book #3).\n    - DELETE — Remove a resource (e.g., DELETE /books/3 to delete book #3).\n\ncURL (pronounced like \"curl\") is a free and open source CLI app for uploading and downloading individual files.\nPut simpler: in almost every internet-connected device (in 2020 also multiplanetary)\n\n                            \n\n\n\n\n\nIn an HTTP request (or response), the header is a collection of key–value pairs that provide metadata \nabout the communication. They do not contain the actual data you’re requesting — instead, they describe \nthe request itself.\n    - \"Accept: application/json\" header telling the server you expect a JSON response.\n    - \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_TOKEN\" header used for authentication (if the API is protected).\n\n\n\nIn HTTP, GET requests usually don’t have a body — they just fetch data. The body is the part after the \nheaders. It contains the actual content you’re sending to the server — usually structured data like JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere is an example from OpenAI guide for API chat completion endpoint:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
